  Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.

  Presence stub for com.google.android.tts ("Speech Recognition & Synthesis",
  formerly "Speech Services by Google"). Android Auto refuses to start without
  a package by this name installed. This package satisfies that check and
  nothing else: it holds no permissions, declares no components, and contains
  no code.

  Deliberately absent, and why. This is the stub where getting it wrong does
  visible damage:

    * No <service> with an android.intent.action.TTS_SERVICE intent filter.
      TextToSpeech engines are discovered by exactly that filter. A stub that
      declared one would be listed in Settings as an available engine, could be
      selected (or auto-selected as the only "Google" engine by an app that
      asks for it by package name), and would then fail to speak. That breaks
      TalkBack, navigation prompts and message read-out across the whole
      device, not just in the car.

    * No <service> with android.speech.RecognitionService. Same argument for
      speech-to-text. /e/OS ships its own offline voice-to-text; do not
      shadow it.

  The correct behaviour of this stub is that the system never notices it. The
  package exists, the TTS subsystem does not see an engine in it, and the
  device's real engine (eSpeak, or the /e/OS offline engine) keeps the job.
  Community reports describe exactly this outcome: TTS "just says the service
  isn't available" inside Android Auto, while the phone itself still speaks.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.google.android.tts"
    android:versionCode="1"
    android:versionName="0.1.0-openaap-stub">

    <uses-sdk android:minSdkVersion="33" android:targetSdkVersion="35" />

    <application
        android:label="Speech services (Android Auto compatibility stub)"
        android:hasCode="false"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:forceQueryable="true" />

</manifest>
